en we're moving these  applications we're trying to i don't say  minimize the amount of changes but  you could imagine if you're a a company  who's used to  you know operating a data center and  you're forced to move 10  000 applications you don't want to make  a ton of changes to things because the  more stuff you're migrating  the more things that can break and not  work and make your cloud journey  sort of more painful so when i think  about  you know how we talk about a lot of  these sort of conversations  it's really in the framework of you know  help me understand how your data center  is  operating today right when we're dealing  with more of like a cloud native company  or a company that was made  you know born in the last few years and  has really developed applications very  differently they're not dealing with  10 000 you know applications that have  been built over the 30 years  um they don't operate a data center  right um just as an example is a kind of  a funny story  i talked with a customer who they  introduced me to their network engineers  and their network engineers told me i've  never configured a router i don't really  know a whole lot about how routing works  yet these are their kind of network  engineers  but because again they're not dealing  with a lot of these antiquated older  constructs you know they just weren't  they weren't familiar with them and they  weren't as concerned about them so  the conversations again were very  different it was more about this is the  service this is this is  how the service actually operates how do  i actually build this  in the environment um and like i said we  weren't really getting into  all these complex details about creating  routing domains and overlaying very  complex security policy over that a lot  of that stuff comes from  just legacy stuff and the way customers  have thought about their data center  right and i think traditionally network  architects and engineers were the ones  who were handling things like  routing and when they were physical  things right and so  it seemed like before they were the ones  who were handling this while not having  as much visibility into what application  developers were doing  um and it kind of reminds me of how  things are starting to change  even when it comes to devops culture for  example um  there's more relationship between  developers and  operators so do you see that happening  with networking  and the application developers or is it  still uh  somewhat of a black box architects and  will it continue to be like that yeah  um i definitely think a lot of customers  still very much  like i'm going to use the term  separation of church and state right um  i work with a very large company on the  west coast and we work with  you know their entire engineering team  and  just as a sort of case in point they  don't even know what applications are  going to come into the environment so  they build everything as sort of a  hybrid cloud environment where  they they present to their developers  and application teams like  here's what services are available in  gcp but here's what services are  available in other clouds  and they leave it up to the application  teams to decide  where they want to deploy right so it  could be in gcp or it could be one of  our competitors right  and this is becoming more and more  common too right where people are  wanting to embrace a multi-cloud server  environment  so they're trying to create kind of an  apples to apples comparison between  these different clouds and the the data  center that they run on-prem so that  when an application team  comes in to run something in the cloud  they don't really care where it runs  whether it runs in their data center  on-prem  whether it runs in uh gcp or runs in one  of our competitors right they leave that  up to the application team to decide  what is the best environment for them  so in that case they are in some ways  completely isolated  and it makes it really interesting  because when we talk to them about you  know what is the future workload look  like and what you know how do you sort  of plan  the stuff one they don't even know right  and two i mean i say this kind of  loosely but they don't really care  right it's kind of like we just build  the infrastructure and we present all  these capabilities to our internal  business units and they make all the  decisions around this stuff  do you think that that's the case for  cloud two because i'm imagining an  application developer  that is working with let's say google  cloud they do have to somewhat have a  fundamental  understanding of how vpcs work and  somewhat know  the operational aspects of it or do you  still think it is  fairly segregated um again i think it  depends on the size of the organization  right the really big organizations with  you know let's say you have 500  traditional network engineers working  for you right it's still a very  segregated conversation  and then i would also say depending on  how we build the infrastructure  the developers don't always even really  even have to have a lot of knowledge  about the underlying infrastructure  right  if the team that we work with does it  the right way  right and they're automating everything  and  they're using a lot of the tools that we  give them and recommend they can  like deploy the environment very quickly  and very seamlessly and then the  developers can come along and  and everything is completely transparent  to them right they can build vms they  can do what developers want to do  right and not have to worry about all of  the  infrastructure components so again if  you kind of do it right you can have the  infrastructure team still being very  walled off  and have the development team come in  and and do their work right  but again to your point the the newer  companies that we deal with when i say  newer you know i think about  you know companies that are doing  service offerings in the cloud right  like maybe that's like their main  business  a lot of times their network engineers  are definitely a lot more attached at  the hip  at the you know with the developers and  with the services that are running  because they may be running just a  handful of services  in the cloud environment it's not like a  like i said before  an engineering team that's like we have  10 000 applications they can't  be like attached to the hip with 10 000  different developers who are working on  all this different stuff it's just  impossible so  i think a lot of your question kind of  depends on how big is the organization  and how much we use the term technical  debt right but how much how much baggage  do they have that they're trying to move  into the cloud environment  yeah and so let's let's drill in on that  a little bit because we're talking about  companies of varying sizes backgrounds  network topology sizes so how does a  path differ for a company that does have  10 000 applications and more of a  traditional networking  complex topology versus a company that  is cloud native  has a service in the cloud already and  they can  kind of get up and run more up and  running more quickly there yeah so  i think the path and i'm going to sort  of go back in history a little bit right  so  four to five years ago i would say you  know a lot of conversations were  we want to dabble with the cloud we want  to move some applications in  um nothing business critical you know i  see a lot of the big organizations that  are still trying to onboard they're  still kind of in that model right  let's let's try to rebuild as much of  the data center as we can  in the cloud okay and i use the term i  like fruit right  it's you know call it like apples to  apples or like apples to oranges right  you know an apple is round and maybe red  and an orange is round but maybe  you know orange you know we're trying to  get them to maybe go from apples to  oranges we don't want to get them to go  from like apples to cherries right i  mean that's a very different fruit it's  much smaller  completely different color uh forgive my  my high level analogy there but  hopefully that makes sense right um you  know because we want them to take some  advantages of the cloud but  asking them to make too much of a change  is just  more than some of these large  enterprises can bite off right like you  don't want to  set yourself on a path to migrate 10 000  things and then find out  you change so many things when you  migrate to cloud that half the  applications don't work they're broken  right you can create a lot of  operational challenges for yourself and  that's where our team comes in like  we're trying to really understand  while we're building networks we're  trying to understand like what are the  applications that run on top of all of  this stuff right because we don't just  build networks in a vacuum right we  build them to support applications  but we also understand you know we're  not just dealing with technology  okay like i can build all sorts of  networks in the cloud  but you've got things like operational  models okay there might be a team of  you know 500 operational engineers who  have to operate this thing every day  and if they operate a certain type of  network that's what they're used to  dealing with and if i sort of take that  network and completely change it too  much  well they got to retrain 500 people  their tools that they use for monitoring  might have to change  right so it's not just about the  technology and how we're using it but  you've got to really consider  you know the applications that we're  moving the operational models the  business structure that the customer  uses  and again this is where things get  really complex with these large  enterprises right  they're businesses they may have 30  different business units that all  operate in a very specific way  if they change things too much and and  the business 