oogle cloud  in a service oriented model yeah so it's  a really good question so if  you look at a traditional networking  model and i have a bunch of different  applications that are running just as an  example  okay typically you were saying this  application is running over here and  this application is running over here  and the two need to talk together  so what is the route to get between them  and what sort of functionality what sort  of security parameter do i have to  overlay on the top of it  but generally speaking we're dealing  with sort of layer three and four  connectivity okay with with a security  bend to it at some level  when you start to talk about service  networking let me talk about one  particular product let me talk about  private service connect or psc as we  call it right  psc is designed to be what we would you  the term i would use as a service  endpoint  right so think about it as an endpoint  that you put into your network  that frontends a service and that could  be a service you're consuming from some  outside  entity it could be a service that you're  consuming from another business unit  but it abstracts a lot of things for you  and  and changes the conversation to how do i  route between here and here it's kind of  just  how do i get to the end point but once  it gets to the end point pse does a lot  of things under the covers that you  don't see it handles the routing  to that other that what we call the  service producer right which again could  be something that lives completely  outside of your organization right it  could be a third party company that  you're buying a service from  so how do you route to that company how  do you handle like overlapping ip space  right if you're  if you're a traditional network engineer  and you're looking at this you're kind  of going okay i've got this ip space  here and this ip space here and i have  to manage it  in some way or i have to control that in  between them  and i'm going to install this device  that's going to do this and again  very network-centric conversation you  introduce psc to the mix and it's like  just  route it to the endpoint and google will  manage everything else we'll manage the  routing  to somebody else in somebody else's  environment completely  will handle all of the natting and all  of that stuff for you so you don't have  to worry about it  so again it just becomes this endpoint  that sits in your network that  front ends the service and we're looking  at doing that you know even with like  google apis and google services where  you can put a psc endpoint in place  right to front-end those apis and you  can control it a little bit different  now one of the things i would say is if  you're a traditional network team and  you're like well okay  what happens when the service doesn't  work how do i troubleshoot this  you know because google is managing a  bunch of the store if you google's  managing some of the routing google's  managing some matting under the covers  you kind of lose visibility to some of  that stuff  right and if you're thinking for a very  network-centric mindset  it kind of causes this knee-jerk  reaction to say like okay  as a network engineer myself somebody  who's been in a situation of like is it  the network that's causing the problem  the network engineers are always trying  to like clear the network and say it's  not the network and you need visibility  end to end  but with something like pse and i'm not  saying pse is a bad thing but you know  once it hits that end point you kind of  lose visibility to it is it something on  the other side that's not working that  you can't see is it the service producer  that's having a problem  you know at the end of the day what  really matters is the service is down  okay but how you troubleshoot it and how  you sort of approach it  is is very different right and i i can  understand hesitation around  relinquishing control or visibility so  that you can troub