ou know a lot of times it's just do it  right and  don't spend the time and the resources  to like re-architect the whole thing  because a lot of these applications  they're not going to be something you're  going to use for 20 years right they're  going to change  so i again i'll go back to that term  technical debt you could move a very  large application in and it brings with  it some technical debt  you could spend a lot of time and money  trying to fix a lot of that stuff  or you can just take the approach of i  know in five years that application's  going to go away anyways  so i don't want to spend the time and  the money to you know refactor  re-architect the application  into a new sort of construct i just know  when we build the next one or whatever  that next application is for that piece  of my business maybe it's your hr  application as an example  it's going to be more cloud native in  the future right it's going to be like  software maybe we're going to go and use  software as a service from somebody else  right and this is going to be a service  that's running in the cloud and maybe  that company is going to use  psc as an example right to  basically put an endpoint into my  network that basically  means my entire hr monolithic  application that i use today  is going to go away in five years  because i'm not using software as a  service  through some other company so when you  think about that  does it really make sense to spend the  time in the effort to do it  in a lot of cases my point of view is it  doesn't right like we can make it work  in the cloud  most times so let's sort of  make it work and let's just move on and  just acknowledge that there's technical  debt there and it maybe is not the  optimal architecture for what you want  right and i think companies probably are  on the same page as you as  you know not wanting to reinvest a whole  lot of upfront capital just to change  the things that are already working  and as we know architectural changes get  new security reviews and  a lot of downstream effects right so do  you have any stories of customers  or people you've worked with that have  had to go through this process  yeah so interesting enough i uh i was  working with one customer  and one of the things people have  understood about cloud is it's a  constantly evolving model and i think  we'll talk about this  in the next segment that we're going to  do right it's not like your traditional  data center right things change much  faster in the cloud  and we'll talk about that of the why but  a lot of enterprises  to change their architecture i actually  had a very senior architect  i mean kind of yelling at me on the  phone and kind of saying like it's great  that google can shift on the fly like  that  but every time we make architectural  changes they have to go through security  reviews there's all of this red tape  that has to be done  so when we think about you know with  this particular customer i was like you  know this is the way the architecture  will look today  in six months or 12 months there's all  these other features are going to come  down the pipe and this is how i would  change it in the future and that's where  it was creating this really kind of  tough rub to say yeah but for me to  implement those changes it's going to  take me six to 12 months because i have  to have security reviews i have to have  architecture reviews  so there was all this red tape right  that sort of comes around with  making these changes to customers so  while we're always kind of saying like  the cloud is constantly evolving you  want to constantly be changing we also  have to be cognizant that  you know google can change on a dime  this is the way google was built  okay but not all enterprises can do that  and that's okay  i think we as a company just have to be  cognizant that not everybody operates at  google  um and i say it's kind of a funny story  because literally they were yelling at  me saying i have to go back to  enterprise 101 school because that's not  how enterprises work right  right right and and i do want to dig  into this a lot more ryan but  we're going to save some of this for the  next segment as you mentioned  um and i want to dive a little bit more  into that  operational shift and what google cloud  specifically is doing  to build a bridge to get people to have  a stepping stone into a service oriented  model  so ryan thank you so much for joining  today and for everybody else  stay tuned we're going to come out with  another episode shortly yep thanks for  having me stephanie  [Music]  you
 

